          %h2#of_chain_complexes Of chain complexes
          %h3#idea Idea
          %p
            Under the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            with strict abelian group structure (modeled by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial
            abelian groups) are identified with non-negatively graded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            of abelian groups
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mo •
                  %mo :
                  %mi SimpAb
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo +
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  N_\bullet : SimpAb \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} Ch_+
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %strong homology groups
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            of abelian groups are the image under this identification of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology+and+cohomology"} chain homology and cohomology
          %p So at least for the case of chain complexes of abelian groups we have the slogan
          .standout
            %p
              %strong homology
              \=
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              under
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
          %p
            Of course historically the development of concepts was precisely the opposite: chain homology is an old fundamental concept in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            that is simpler to deal with than
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+groups"} simplicial homotopy groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            useful after all.
          %p
            Conceptually, however, it can be useful to understand homology as a special kind of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
            picture:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            derives its name from that fact that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology+and+cohomology"} chain homology and cohomology
            are dual concepts. But later generalizations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            and further to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            showed that the restricted notion of homology is an insufficient dual model for cohomology: what cohomology is really dual to is the more general concept of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomotopy"} cohomotopy
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+duality"} Eckmann-Hilton duality
          %h3#definition Definition
          %p
            The category of abelian groups is in particular an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            and their homology in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            and let
